SUMMARY:Clay Exploration Evenings: Lvl 2\, 9/24 - 10/29 — Sebastopol Cen
 ter For the Arts\, California
DTSTART:20260924T000000
DTEND:20260925T000000
UID:afc6fe817d4e839ced5ea394691acdd5@sebarts.org
DESCRIPTION:Clay Exploration Evenings: Lvl 2\, 9/24 - 10/29\n\n  \n    \n 
      \n        \n          $255.00\n        \n      \n    \n  \n\nThursday
 s\, September 24 - October 29\, 2026 (6 sessions) 6 - 8:30pm$210 + $45 cla
 y fee (pre-registration required)This course is designed for students wish
 ing to explore hand-building techniques at an intermediate to advanced lev
 el and delve deeper into refining their clay skills. Each session will sta
 rt with a demonstration of a specific technique related to functional and/
 or decorative pottery. Participants are invited to explore new ideas and t
 o try pieces that might be of a more challenging nature. Some of what we c
 an explore are freeform coil pots\, soft slab work\, hard slab technique\,
  a variety of sculptural techniques and a sampling of the many stamping\, 
 carving and textural surface treatments on clay. We will investigate ways 
 to use slips and glaze that will add color to the forms we create.About th
 e Instructor: Michael Citrino has had a love affair with clay for over 50 
 years. After his college experience at California College of Arts and Craf
 ts with Viola Frey and further work at Humboldt State University with Lew 
 Merrick\, he ran a stained glass business for 18 years. Michael and his wi
 fe left for Turkey in 1991 to teach in International Schools. For 26 years
  he taught K-12 art\, including Ceramics\, Drawing\, Photography\, Design 
 and Sculpture at schools in six different countries. He started clay progr
 ams for High School and Middle School students at three of those schools. 
 At four of these schools he offered after school and weekend ceramics sess
 ions for teachers and parents. Michael’s work ranges from the traditiona
 l to the experimental and a wide swath in between. His approach to teachin
 g is to help students explore the playful nature of the clay room and nurt
 ure a love for the possibilities that medium presents. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\
 n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n          \n            \n 
